WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.160
THE STATE OF EBAY:
Aside from Amazon, eBay is likely the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:09.160
well known online retailer in the entire world.&nbsp;
They been around for nearly 30 years and they rank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:15.320
as the 23rd most visited website in the US pulling&nbsp;
in more than 600 million visits every single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:21.440
month. For perspective, that’s nearly double&nbsp;
the visits that Netflix gets. But legacy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.760
popularity are pretty much the only things that&nbsp;
eBay still has going for them. Behind the scenes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:33.040
eBay has been facing a decline for basically this&nbsp;
entire century. In fact, the only time that eBay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:37.600
stock has seen a significant surge over the&nbsp;
past 25 years was when they decided to spin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:43.360
off PayPal into its own business in 2015. Before&nbsp;
that, eBay stock was mostly flat, and after that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:48.440
eBay stock has been mostly flat as well aside&nbsp;
from the pandemic boost which has completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:54.120
dissipated. For contrast, here’s how Amazon’s&nbsp;
stock graph looks over the same time period. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.120 --> 00:01:00.200
when we take a look at the company’s fundamentals,&nbsp;
it’s not surprising why. eBay’s revenue today is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:07.600
about the same as it was back in 2010. And things&nbsp;
only look worse in terms of net income. As recent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:13.720
as last year, eBay had a negative 12 month&nbsp;
period where they lost $1.2 billion. Since then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:18.120
they have recovered back into the green, but&nbsp;
again, the numbers really aren’t all that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:22.880
much better than they were back in 2010. The one&nbsp;
thing that eBay has going for them is that their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:31.160
net margins of 27% are leagues ahead of Amazon who&nbsp;
sits at just 3.62%. However, it doesn’t seem like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:36.840
investors really care much about this as their&nbsp;
far more optimistic about Amazon’s future. That’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:44.840
why Amazon still has a PE ratio of 80 while eBay&nbsp;
only has a PE ratio of 8. Even IBM has a PE ratio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:50.640
of 21 meaning that investors are almost 3 times&nbsp;
more optimistic about IBM than eBay. And when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:55.880
you consider that many analysts consider eBay&nbsp;
to be all but dead, their lackluster valuation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.880 --> 00:02:08.424
isn’t all that surprising, but it does leave the&nbsp;
question of what in the world happened to eBay?

00:02:08.424 --> 00:02:09.480
UNHAPPY SELLERS:
When you scour the web&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:14.720
for concerns regarding eBay, one of the biggest&nbsp;
concerns seems to be that sales are falling off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:20.440
a cliff. And this sentiment has especially&nbsp;
picked up over the past 6 months. There’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:25.080
bunch of prevalent theories floating around.&nbsp;
For example, student loan payments restarted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:29.800
recently meaning that a lot of people now have&nbsp;
less disposable income. Inflation and high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:34.400
interest rates have also been been eating into&nbsp;
people’s savings. Some sellers even speculate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:39.680
that the sales drought may have been caused by an&nbsp;
eBay algorithm change in April. On the surface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:45.640
it seems that a lot of these explanations make&nbsp;
sense but the evidence doesn’t exactly back up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:51.200
these theories. For example, Amazon and Walmart&nbsp;
are still posting record revenue numbers. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:55.760
eBay themselves are posting relatively&nbsp;
flat revenue. Not to mention, concerns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:01.320
about falling sales seems to be a constant theme&nbsp;
throughout history. In basically any given year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:06.560
you can go onto reddit and find a bunch of sellers&nbsp;
claiming that sales are worse than ever. With that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:11.840
being said though, that doesn’t necessarily mean&nbsp;
that they’re wrong. The reality is that selling on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:19.840
eBay is harder than ever but not because of macro&nbsp;
economic reasons but competition. Back in 2009,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:26.880
eBay only had a total of 2.1 million annual active&nbsp;
sellers. Today, eBay has a total of 18.3 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:33.040
annual active sellers or 9 times more. It’s&nbsp;
a similar case with Amazon but there’s one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:39.800
crucial difference. Amazon’s revenue has grown&nbsp;
22X since 2009. eBay’s revenue on the other had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:47.120
has only increased 15%. And a lot of that can&nbsp;
be explained by consistently increasing fees.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:51.840
If we take a look at the gross merchandize volume&nbsp;
going through eBay, we can see that the figure is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:58.800
lower today than it was back in 2013. And that’s&nbsp;
not even accounting for inflation. Since 2009,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:03.840
prices have gone up by 43% meaning that flat&nbsp;
revenue could actually be indicative of up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:09.760
to 30% lower unit sales. And given that eBay’s&nbsp;
overall transactional revenue itself is down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:16.280
eBay’s overall unit sales could actually be down&nbsp;
by as much as 40%. When you combine 40% lower unit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:21.760
sales with 9 times the number of sellers, it’s no&nbsp;
wonder why sellers are always complaining about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:27.400
sales going down. The reality is that it could&nbsp;
be as much 10 to 15 times harder to get a sale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:33.600
today than it was back in 2009, and all of that&nbsp;
is just on the seller side of things. The buyer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:39.120
side of things don’t look any better as eBay has&nbsp;
been shedding users like the plague. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:44.320
they didn’t even enjoy bounce during the pandemic.&nbsp;
Their peak user count was back in 2018 when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:51.240
had 175 million annual users. Today, that number&nbsp;
stands at 129 million users or nearly 50 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:57.160
less. And this is of course anecdotal but if you&nbsp;
Google “I will never buy from eBay again”, you get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:04.280
dozens upon dozens of links. That’s not how it is&nbsp;
with Amazon or Walmart or even Craigslist. Sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:10.360
they have few threads like that here and there but&nbsp;
with eBay, it’s basically ubiquitous. And when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:16.080
combine this anecdotal evidence with the fact that&nbsp;
eBay is down nearly 50 million buyers, its clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:21.720
that these threads aren’t just from disgruntled&nbsp;
customers. Rather, it seems to be a serious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:27.240
problem with eBay as a whole which brings us into&nbsp;
the question of why are customers so mad at eBay?

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:36.000
UNHAPPY BUYERS:
Reading through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:41.560
dozens of negative threads about eBay, it seems&nbsp;
that by far the biggest complaint from buyers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:47.360
is prevalence of scams on eBay. You know, not&nbsp;
receiving the product, counterfeit products,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:52.520
damaged products, empty boxes, fake tracking&nbsp;
numbers, and so on. Most of these scams are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:57.640
covered by eBay’s money back guarantee policy,&nbsp;
but going through that process is usually not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.640 --> 00:06:02.840
all that fun even if you do end up getting your&nbsp;
money back. So, naturally, buyers are preferring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:07.880
to shop at other retailers where scams are much&nbsp;
less prevalent like Amazon which brings me into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:15.000
the primary difference between Amazon and eBay:&nbsp;
FBA. If you’re not familiar with FBA, it stands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:20.480
for Fulfilled By Amazon and it’s basically the&nbsp;
bread and butter of the entire site. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:26.080
Amazon isn’t just a marketplace like eBay or&nbsp;
Craiglists, it’s much more of a traditional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:31.440
retailer as they take on the entire responsibility&nbsp;
of customer service. Everything from distributing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:35.360
inventory across the country, routing orders,&nbsp;
labeling, and 2 day shipping to handling returns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:40.960
covering damages, and customer service. That’s&nbsp;
what Amazon FBA offers. The only thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:46.640
sellers have to do on Amazon is create a listing&nbsp;
and send inventory to Amazon and Amazon will take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:51.880
care of the rest. Now technically, sellers can&nbsp;
still elect to do all of this themselves but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:58.040
94% of sellers just go through Amazon FBA. And&nbsp;
the 6% of sellers that don’t use Amazon FBA are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:03.320
usually big brands who can handle it themselves.&nbsp;
Ironically, even a lot of big brands like Samsung&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:08.800
prefer to just go through Amazon FBA themselves.&nbsp;
What this all means is that you virtually never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:15.280
interact with the seller on Amazon, you basically&nbsp;
only interact with Amazon. Amazon even goes as far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:20.360
as taking responsibility for shipping and&nbsp;
order related mishaps. For example, if a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:25.800
customer leaves a poor review because of a lost or&nbsp;
damaged package, not only will Amazon remove the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:30.640
negative review but they will reimburse both the&nbsp;
buyer and seller because it was Amazon’s fault,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:36.520
not the buyer or sellers. And that’s why the&nbsp;
service is so consistent and reliable especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:41.760
in comparison to eBay where it truly is just a&nbsp;
marketplace of buyers and sellers. The quality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:46.280
of service you receive is largely dependent&nbsp;
on how much the given seller cares and it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:51.680
largely up to you to look out for yourself. eBay&nbsp;
did launch their own fulfillment service in mid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:58.040
2019 but it never quite caught on like Amazon FBA&nbsp;
did because eBay sellers often vastly different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:03.480
goals. A bunch of eBay listings are just people&nbsp;
selling one off items that they no longer need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:09.080
for which a fulfillment service doesn’t really&nbsp;
make sense. A fulfillment service makes much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:13.360
sense if you’re trying to build a full on business&nbsp;
around a given product that you plan on selling in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:19.400
large quantities. So, with that being said, I&nbsp;
don’t think eBay’s consistency and reliability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:29.240
will be improving anytime soon which brings one&nbsp;
the root question which is: why even use eBay?

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:35.680
A DYING PLATFORM:
Taking a step back, the biggest problem with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:41.400
eBay is that they offer no real value for what&nbsp;
they charge. They’re over hear charging sellers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:47.920
15% on a bunch of categories just like Amazon does&nbsp;
but they don’t offer any of the perks that Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:53.840
does. They don’t have the same reliability, the&nbsp;
reputation, or the convenience. They’re more like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:59.880
a flea market that truly just connects buyers&nbsp;
and sellers which in itself isn’t a bad thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:05.840
There’s definitely a place for this sort of market&nbsp;
online, but if this truly is just a flea market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:10.760
that doesn’t come with any of the protctions that&nbsp;
Amazon on the buyer side or on the seller side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:16.840
why even bother with eBay? eBay is essentially&nbsp;
charging Amazon or Walmart level fees while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:22.120
offering Craigslist or Facebook marketplace&nbsp;
level of service. If this was a new business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:27.760
eBay wouldn’t even stand a chance, but since&nbsp;
they’re established and have brand recognition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:33.200
a lot of people still shop on eBay from time to&nbsp;
time until they get burned after which they never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:38.200
return. The people who want more reliability and&nbsp;
convenience just switch to buying and selling on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:43.400
Amazon while the people who don’t care to have a&nbsp;
deadweight middle party like eBay just switch to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:49.760
Craigslist. The only unique reason to come to eBay&nbsp;
at this point is their auctions which themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:54.080
have a bunch of shortfalls like people placing&nbsp;
bids that they have no intention of following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:59.680
through with. And I think all of these relative&nbsp;
shortfalls can be explained by the fundamentally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:05.800
different business models of Amazon, eBay, and&nbsp;
Craigslist. Jeff Bezos has always believed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:11.640
other people’s margin is his opportunity. This&nbsp;
is how he built up Amazon and its still how it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:17.560
runs today. That’s why Amazon is able to offer&nbsp;
so much value for the prices they charge. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:24.520
simply just don’t make money. Remember how we&nbsp;
talked about Amazon’s margins being 3.62%? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.520 --> 00:10:30.600
that’s with AWS propping up their retail numbers.&nbsp;
Without AWS, Amazon’s operating margin is closer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:37.360
to 1.8% and it’s often negative actually. The&nbsp;
reality is that Amazon doesn’t care all that much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:42.280
about making money from theri retail operation.&nbsp;
Their retail business is more about market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:48.520
control, brand awareness, and influence. They’re&nbsp;
real money maker is AWS. Meanwhile, Craigslist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:54.360
doesn’t really care at all to maximize profits.&nbsp;
They’re almost run like a non-profit with very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.360 --> 00:11:00.840
modest profits and they have no plans of changing.&nbsp;
So, eBay is basically competing against two zero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:07.000
margin businesses and they’re getting squeezed&nbsp;
from both sides. And the more time that pases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:12.480
the more apparent eBay’s 27% margin becomes in&nbsp;
comparison to the competition. The worst part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:19.280
is that there is no obvious solution. They don’t&nbsp;
have an AWS that they can rely on for profits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:25.080
Actually technically, they did use to have all of&nbsp;
PayPal but that’s no longer the case. And I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:31.320
it’s safe to assume that eBay also does not want&nbsp;
to become a quasi non profit like Craigslist. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:36.520
they’re kind of stuck between a rock and hard&nbsp;
place. The only way they can really move forward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.520 --> 00:11:41.960
is by sacrificing profits and becoming more&nbsp;
competitive but that’s not something they really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.960 --> 00:11:47.440
want to do. So instead, they’ve basically just&nbsp;
decided to enjoy the traffic and profits while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:54.440
they last which should still be for quite some&nbsp;
time. When things become unsustainable, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.440 --> 00:12:00.800
they’ll deal with it then. And that right there&nbsp;
is the sad state of eBay. They’ve been reduced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:05.400
from being one of the most dominant online&nbsp;
retailers to resorting to milking sellers as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:10.920
much as possible while they still can. Unlike&nbsp;
eBay, Craigslist has a much happier ending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:15.360
as they truly know who they are and what they&nbsp;
stand for. Check out this video to learn more.

